# The collector's cost on the wake path — LINUX, 2026-09-09
#
# lima flint-nfs-client: aarch64, kernel 6.8.0-138-generic, 2 vCPU.
# Cross-built aarch64-unknown-linux-musl, run through the REAL
# reclaim_at_rest (the load-bearing path), n=2.
#
#   time ~= 2 + 0.5 x packs + 0.0063 x objects   (ms)
#
# vs darwin/arm64, same code, same probe:
#   time ~= 26 + 12.9 x packs + 0.005 x objects   (ms)
#
# The PACK term is 25x cheaper on Linux (0.5 vs 12.9 ms) — it is a
# fork, and macOS forks slowly. The OBJECT term is NOT cheaper
# (6.3 vs 5 us) because it is CPU: rev-list output, show-index
# output, and 33k object ids allocated as Strings and hashed.
# REFS ARE FREE on both, to 2,049.
#
# A holder-count rewrite of the greedy changed NOTHING (239.4 -> 237.2):
# all() short-circuits, so with a chain of packs the scan already
# exited on the first object. The predicted O(objects x packs) term
# is not reached by this workload. Kept anyway — it costs nothing
# measurable and bounds the COLLECTING case, which is UNMEASURED.

